(1) Translation:

Wei Wuhou (魏武侯) had planned affairs and they turned out well; none of his assembled ministers could match him. He withdrew from court wearing a pleased expression. Wu Qi (吳起) stepped forward and said: "Have you ever heard the words of King Zhuang of Chu (楚莊王) from those around you, sir?" Wuhou said: "What were the words of King Zhuang of Chu?" Wu Qi replied: "King Zhuang of Chu planned affairs and they turned out well; none of his ministers could match him. He withdrew from court wearing a worried expression. Shen Gong Wu Chen (申公巫臣) stepped forward and asked: 'The king has come from court wearing a worried expression — why?' King Zhuang said: 'I, the unworthy one, planned affairs and they turned out well; none of my ministers could match me. This is why I am worried. In the words of Zhong Hui (中蘬) it is said: "A lord who of himself finds a teacher becomes king; one who finds a friend becomes hegemon; one who finds one who will question him survives; one who plans by himself and finds none his equal perishes." Now, with my own unworthiness, none of my ministers can match me — is my state not close to perishing? This is why I am worried.' King Zhuang of Chu was worried, while you, my lord, are pleased." Wuhou drew back, bowed twice, and said: "Heaven has sent you, master, to shake me out of my faults."

(2) Moral of the story:

The story's lesson is that a ruler should fear, not celebrate, being surrounded by people who cannot match or correct him. King Zhuang of Chu treats his own superiority over his ministers as a symptom of danger — a sign that no one will check his errors, and the state is near ruin. Wei Wuhou, by contrast, takes pleasure in the same situation, and Wu Qi uses the parallel to rebuke him. The moral is that the greatest peril for a leader is the absence of worthy critics and counselors; contentment with being unchallenged is the beginning of decline. A good ruler welcomes those who can teach, befriend, or question him, and fears flattery and isolation. (The passage also illustrates the value of blunt remonstrance and the ruler's willingness to accept correction — Wuhou's bow shows the ideal response.)

(3) Western counterparts:

Yes, several concepts resonate with Western (especially classical and Christian) thought:

- The danger of the flatterer and the necessity of frank counsel: This is a major theme in Cicero (De Amicitia, on flattery vs. true friendship), in Plutarch's essays on how to tell a flatterer from a friend, and in the "mirror for princes" tradition (e.g., Erasmus' Education of a Christian Prince, Machiavelli's warnings about flatterers in The Prince, chapter 23). The idea that a ruler is lost when no one dares contradict him is close to Machiavelli's advice to avoid flatterers and to choose wise counselors.

- "Find a teacher/friend" as the basis of rule: Plato's Republic and the Seventh Letter stress the need for a ruler to be advised by philosophers or wise counselors; Aristotle's Politics discusses friendship (philia) as essential to the city and to the ruler. The tripartite scheme (teacher → king, friend → hegemon, questioner → survival) has a rough analogue in the classical concern with parrhesia (frank speech) and in the Stoic idea that the wise man needs friends and teachers.

- Self-sufficiency as a path to ruin: The Greek idea of hubris and the tragic overreach of the self-sufficient ruler (e.g., Creon in Sophocles' Antigone) parallels the warning that "one who plans by himself and finds none his equal perishes." Christian thought adds the theme of pride and the fall (e.g., Augustine on pride as the root of sin; the proverb "pride goes before a fall").

- The ruler's fear as a sign of virtue: The notion that a good ruler is anxious about his own inadequacy rather than pleased with his superiority has analogues in Christian humility (e.g., the "royal" virtue of humility in Aquinas) and in the classical ideal of the modest statesman (e.g., Cincinnatus, or Plato's philosopher-king who does not desire power).

- Remonstrance and the "shaking out of faults": The idea that a counselor is a "gift of heaven" sent to correct the ruler resembles the Western tradition of the king's conscience, the court preacher, and the "jester" or "truth-teller" figure (e.g., the fool in Shakespeare, or the prophet Nathan confronting David in the Bible).

Caveats: The Chinese emphasis on the ruler's moral self-cultivation and on the cosmic/ritual dimension of kingship differs from the more juridical and institutional Western approaches (e.g., constitutional checks, "mixed government" in Polybius). The specific tripartite ranking (teacher/friend/questioner) has no exact Western equivalent, though it is structurally similar to the classical distinctions among types of counsel and friendship. [end of text]


